{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And now, if You will bear their sin</b> — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 32:32): \"If You will bear their sin — well and good, and I do not say to You, 'blot me out'; but if not, blot me out.\" This is an elliptical verse, and there are many like it. <b>From Your book</b> — from the entire Torah, so that they should not say about me that I am unworthy to seek mercy on their behalf. If so, what reply did the Holy One, blessed be He, give him: \"Whoever has sinned against Me, I will blot him out of My book\" — and there is no other to be blotted out of His book. And perhaps one might say: I blot out only whoever has sinned against Me, and you have not sinned against Me — but this is not correct.\n\nAnd Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 32:32) that <b>\"from Your book which You have written\"</b> is like <b>\"the court sat, and the books were opened\"</b> (Daniel 7:10), and the \"books,\" in his view, are the arrangements of the heavens upon which the decrees of the lower realm depend. And He said, \"Whoever has sinned against Me, I will blot him out of My book,\" to say: I will not blot you out, but I will blot out from the people the sinners who sinned against Me in their intent and were not slain — and that is what is written (Exodus 32:35), <b>\"And YHWH struck the people\"</b>.\n\nAnd this is not correct in my eyes, for besides those slain by the sword of the Levites and those who died in the plague, the greater part of the congregation were sinners against Him, as I have written (Ramban on Exodus 32:7). And according to my opinion, Moses said, \"And now, if You will bear their sin\" — in Your mercy, \"and if not, blot me out, I pray You, from Your book of life,\" and I will suffer their punishment myself — as Scripture says (Isaiah 53:5), <b>\"But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was crushed for our iniquities; the chastisement of our peace was upon him, and with his stripes we are healed.\"</b> And the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: The one who sins — I will blot out of My book, and not you, for you have not sinned."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "In the opening, Ramban quotes Rashi's interpretation that the verse is elliptical (מקרא קצר); the logic is that Moses left unstated the intermediate case ('if You will bear their sin — good; if not — blot me out')."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:20:30.963735037Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}